column24:
A contractor was attempting to swing from the boat to the platform. When his feet landed on the boat landing, he was not able to keep his balance and fell backwards into the water. He was immediately retrieved by the boat and platform personnel. There were no injuries and no issues following this event.

ADDITIONAL INFORMATION:
-7 other personnel had successfully made swing rope transfers prior to this incident.
-Seas were approximately 2-4' at the time.

CORRECTIVE ACTIONS: 
The BSEE Safety Alert on Swing Rope incidents was distributed to all manned platforms for review in their next safety meeting.

UPDATE:
-Contractor is not an option in the dropdown box above for Contractors;
-The contractor was retrieved from the water by the personnel on site using the boat's rescue ladder;
-Following the event he was transferred back to the platform where he and the crew were sleeping (SS 193) and he changed clothes and was then flown to meet his crew at SS 182-C (this occurred within 2 hours following the event);
-There was no first aid administered since there were no injuries;
-It should be noted that the contractor stated, once he landed in the water, he swam away from the boat;
-Multiple Statements and Photos have been attached to this report.

Employee was on a 4' well stand greasing and working the B-18 casing valve when a 1" Stainless Steel Tubing Line parted. He fell off the stand and onto the grating. He sustained injuries and was evacuated and treated at a medical facility. The incident is under investigation.

NOTE: BSEE-New Orleans was notified soon after the incident and has visited the facility.

UPDATE:
The investigation for this incident is ongoing. 

UPDATE2:
The following information was sent on 4/10/23:
The investigation is ongoing but we have made significant progress. I left voicemail messages with each of you but below is a summary of what has been completed and what is planned.
. The injured individual is recovering and is presently in physical therapy;
. The investigation team has met with the injured person's company;
. The Investigation team has conducted 7 interviews with personnel to date;
. Members of the investigation team will make a visit to Contractor company this week;
. Members of the investigation team plan to make a second offshore trip;
. Once the investigation findings and corrective actions are established they will complete the report.

UPDATE:
The Incident Investigation Report was uploaded into eWell on 5/25/23.

1/16/2024 Update;
Confirmed final quick share report was attached, selected crane instead of other lifting device and submitted as final report.

1/3/2024 Update; 
Which crane and its location was involved in this incident? Riser elevator-horizontal (RE-H) crane was being used and is located on the aft deck in riser hold area.
What was the sea state & wind speed at the time of the incident? Seas were 5-8' and 22-27 knt winds 220 degrees.

While landing a riser joint in the saddle with the RE-H (Riser Elevator Horizontal), the Spotter called an all stop when an abnormal sound was heard and witnessed the riser joint contact the handrail on the port side saddle walkway. Once the joint was confirmed to be secured in the saddle; the Crane Operator checked and confirmed that the fiberglass shim was protruding which allowed contact with the handrail. Vessel motion hindered personnel from inspecting riser on saddle walkway. A visual inspection was conducted, no broken pieces on the shim was observed. The Crane Operator began hoisting riser from cradle with the ROC(Riser Overhead Crane), a piece of fiberglass shim on the external housing was observed descending from riser joint, coming to rest at the base of the stairs in the lower riser hull. Another piece was found on the port walkway around the cradle. All Stop called rig management notified. No injuries reported from the incident. The riser hull is a Permeant Red Zone, all personnel were in the designated step back as per the JBD. A Temp Black Zone was established for the lay down area of the riser hull. The fiberglass riser shim weighed 0.5lbs and feel 38' to the hull floor.

MC254 A - RUE OCS-G 30372_x000D_
@0516 hrs: General Alarm over platform Gaitronics sounded. All control room was showing was that the PCM module tripped knocking down the Subsea Pump. _x000D_
Everyone mustered at their respective stations._x000D_
@0517 hrs: Fire Teams called they were donning gear and would be on standby while On Scene Commander was asked by Incident Commander to assess the PCM Building where there was a process trip. (This is the first time we have had a platform annunciated alarm via Gaitronics regarding this building)._x000D_
@0519 hrs: Galley Crew reported Main and Temporary Quarters were cleared._x000D_
On Scene Command said it was very hot inside of the building but there was no fire or smoke detected._x000D_
Incident Commander had On Scene Commander request just Fire team 1 to come to PCM Building and standby on location._x000D_
Incident Commander then asked 2 of the 3 I/E's to go to PCM building and verify there was no issue other than it got hot enough in there to shut down Kosmos SSP._x000D_
@0520 hrs: The Primary Muster Area reported a full Muster._x000D_
@0522 hrs: Fire Team 1 was on standby monitoring Kosmos PCM Building in case signs of a fire became present._x000D_
I/E's confirmed it was just that the A/C's had tripped and the building reached its high temperature shutdown to protect the electrical equipment._x000D_
@0524 hrs: due to full muster being called in it was found to be no safety issue, OIM gave an All Clear and to stand down. _x000D_
It was found out later this morning that if the subsea pump was stopped it was set to trigger the General Alarm.  It has now been corrected.
